.venv/
# Session state of the coding agents that work in this tree: tool scratch, not
# project content, and rewriting it on every turn would keep the branch dirty.
.omo/
.codegraph
.freebuff/
AGENTS.md
.project_id
*.egg-info/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
artifacts/*
!artifacts/study-summaries/
# Local proof run (2026-09-13): the machine-checked rehearsal record - the
# verdict and session summary are durable evidence; the heavy checkpoint and
# dataset trees stay local scratch.
!artifacts/local-proof/
artifacts/local-proof/*
!artifacts/local-proof/verdict.json
!artifacts/local-proof/session-summary.json
# Real-data protocol proof (TinyStories through the section 5.1 machinery):
# the machine-checked verdict and session summary are durable evidence; the
# checkpoint/dataset scratch stays local.
!artifacts/real-proof/
artifacts/real-proof/*
!artifacts/real-proof/verdict.json
!artifacts/real-proof/session-summary.json
# Real-LM training record: the machine-checked report is durable evidence;
# the corpus (1.9 GB), token stream, and checkpoints stay local scratch.
!artifacts/real-lm/
artifacts/real-lm/*
!artifacts/real-lm/report.json
# The code-LM leg's cross-machine rows (2026-09-25): two of the four rows the
# comparison table is made of, measured by scripts/push_code_bench.py on Kaggle's T4 at
# the same protocol and the same 4 MB held-out prefix. They are committed because the
# journal quotes their numbers and because a run that spent GPU quota should leave an
# artifact rather than scrollback - the driver used to delete the directory they arrived
# in. The local card scores all four models so HumanEval's sampled decoding is not split
# across devices, so these are a check on the protocol, not a table input. 116 KB.
!artifacts/code-lm/
artifacts/code-lm/*
!artifacts/code-lm/bench/
artifacts/code-lm/bench/*
!artifacts/code-lm/bench/kaggle/
artifacts/code-lm/bench/kaggle/*
!artifacts/code-lm/bench/kaggle/*.json
# The TPU probe's record (2026-09-25): the one measurement the pre-registered port rule
# reads was missing from it, and the four phases that did run are the numbers quoted in
# the journal - including the per-core rates that sit below the rate eight cores would
# need to clear the rule's own 2x bar. Committed because the entry quotes them, and
# because the fetch that produced it deleted its own output for three days, which is how
# a `COMPLETE` kernel went unread. 2 KB; the kernel and its logs stay scratch.
!artifacts/tpu-probe/
artifacts/tpu-probe/*
!artifacts/tpu-probe/output/
artifacts/tpu-probe/output/*
# Named by the kernel version that produced it, not "the latest record": a fetch lands in
# this directory and would otherwise overwrite the evidence under it. Each record is added
# here when the journal cites its numbers, which is what makes them re-readable. v5 is the
# run whose child process fixed v4's runtime precondition and then died in libtpu's
# topology; the two together are what the aggregate measurement's redesign reads from.
!artifacts/tpu-probe/output/tpu-probe-v4.json
!artifacts/tpu-probe/output/tpu-probe-v5.json
# v6 (2026-09-26) completed the registered five-phase probe and returned PORT; its
# eight-chip aggregate is the first record the decision rule can actually evaluate.
!artifacts/tpu-probe/output/tpu-probe-v6.json
!artifacts/tpu-probe/output/act-tpu-probe-v6.log
# The held-out stream the real-LM proof is scored on (2026-09-25). A perplexity
# with nothing beside it proves nothing - 5.5 could be good or terrible - so the
# record carries reference baselines, and those are recomputed from this file.
# 14 KB, versus a 1.9 GB corpus and a 250 MB checkpoint that stay local: it is
# the one piece that makes the proof checkable without either.
!artifacts/real-lm/val-tokens.npy
# Architecture-search record (2026-09-17). Every file here is load-bearing:
# the per-seed ledgers are the primary data (regenerating them costs GPU
# hours), `zoo_definition.json` is needed by scripts/arch_zoo_analyze.py to
# join structural axes onto old ledgers, and tests pin the phase's headline
# result by reading those ledgers. Only the progress logs are scratch.
!artifacts/ARCH_SEARCH_FINDINGS.md
!artifacts/arch-zoo/
artifacts/arch-zoo/*
!artifacts/arch-zoo/PERFORMANCE_NOTES.md
!artifacts/arch-zoo/SCREENING_AUDIT.md
!artifacts/arch-zoo/analysis.md
!artifacts/arch-zoo/zoo_definition.json
!artifacts/arch-zoo/zoo_results.json
# One block per family, not per experiment: the durable filenames are the same in
# every arch-zoo arm, and a new arm that needs a .gitignore edit before its
# evidence is committable is a new arm whose evidence gets silently dropped.
!artifacts/arch-zoo-*/
artifacts/arch-zoo-*/*
!artifacts/arch-zoo-*/NOTES.md
!artifacts/arch-zoo-*/decision.md
!artifacts/arch-zoo-*/ranked.json
!artifacts/arch-zoo-*/search_cost.json
!artifacts/arch-zoo-*/matching_report.json
!artifacts/arch-zoo-*/distance_matrix.json
!artifacts/arch-zoo-*/zoo_definition.json
!artifacts/arch-zoo-*/*_seed*_results.jsonl
!artifacts/mixer-benchmark/
artifacts/mixer-benchmark/*
!artifacts/mixer-benchmark/FINDINGS.md
!artifacts/mixer-benchmark/mixer_throughput.json
# Campaign session logs (2026-09-22): the platform's own log for a session that ran to
# completion, which is the only measurement anywhere of what the exit publish costs
# (25.42 GiB in 12 min) and of the child-output buffering that starved four failure
# captures. A log is evidence for a number here, so the filename pattern is stable -
# `session-<first>-<last>-log.json` - for the same reason the arch-zoo arm files are.
!artifacts/v0-session-logs/
artifacts/v0-session-logs/*
!artifacts/v0-session-logs/NOTES.md
!artifacts/v0-session-logs/session-*-log.json
# Per-session evidence kept by the driver since 2026-09-27: the platform serves
# `kaggle kernels logs` for the *latest* version only, so a session's kernel log,
# live capture and runner summary are copied here at its own end rather than
# fetched later (when the next push has already replaced them). The kernel-log
# and session-log names already match the glob above; the live capture is text.
!artifacts/v0-session-logs/session-*-live.log
# The completed Gate-4 primary session (kernel v7, 2026-09-26): keep its compact
# machine-readable queue, benchmark, publish and per-seed audit records, plus the
# child's tee used to reconcile the live capture. The multi-gigabyte checkpoints
# remain on Kaggle; these records total only tens of KB.
!artifacts/v0-session-logs/session-15024-26-session.json
!artifacts/v0-session-logs/session-15024-26-publish.json
!artifacts/v0-session-logs/session-15024-26-benchmark.json
!artifacts/v0-session-logs/session-15024-26-child-stream.log
!artifacts/v0-session-logs/session-15024-summary.json
!artifacts/v0-session-logs/session-15025-summary.json
!artifacts/v0-session-logs/session-15026-summary.json
# The completed Gate-4 primary session (kernel v11, 2026-09-27): the first session whose
# restore worked (three seeds skipped, three trained, 6/12 published), and the last one
# nobody watched - the workstation rebooted while the driver was polling and the session
# finished and published on Kaggle by itself. Its platform log, the runner's own
# session-log, the publish receipt, the benchmark and the child's tee are the whole record;
# there is no live capture because the reboot destroyed the driver's work directory. Its
# per-seed records are also what exposed the provenance hole: the published 15024-15026 are
# v10's retrained copies, and every manifest in the dataset says `code_revision: unknown`.
!artifacts/v0-session-logs/session-15027-29-session.json
!artifacts/v0-session-logs/session-15027-29-publish.json
!artifacts/v0-session-logs/session-15027-29-benchmark.json
!artifacts/v0-session-logs/session-15027-29-child-stream.log
!artifacts/v0-session-logs/session-15027-summary.json
!artifacts/v0-session-logs/session-15028-summary.json
!artifacts/v0-session-logs/session-15029-summary.json
# The two-seed concurrency rehearsal (2026-09-22): the blocker on the null queue's
# two-seeds-per-pair amendment, which the journal prices at 8.49 GPU-h and which is also the
# bar-level route to a one-week block. The record is the evidence - it carries both
# scenarios' full result payloads, so the comparison can be re-run from the artifact alone -
# and the two checkpoint trees stay local scratch.
!artifacts/null-pair-rehearsal/
artifacts/null-pair-rehearsal/*
!artifacts/null-pair-rehearsal/rehearsal.json
# The section 5.14 clause-power reading (2026-09-20, extended 2026-09-22): the artifact is
# the record the journal names as where the reading lives, and
# `tests/test_null_clause_power.py` asserts the committed file equals a fresh
# `build_report()`. It was left ignored by the blanket `artifacts/*` rule rather than by
# any decision, so the test could only pass on the machine that had generated it.
!artifacts/null-clause-power.json
# The raw depth-1 gate sweep behind the factor map (2026-09-12): report section 9.12
# cites it as the record showing the probe controller never ran in the legacy study,
# and `tests/test_factor_map_recheck.py` reads its first line to reproduce the
# published seed. It was left ignored by the blanket `artifacts/*` rule rather than by
# any decision, which is the same defect `null-clause-power.json` had - a test that
# could only pass on the machine that generated the file. 10 MB, and load-bearing:
# committing it is the option that keeps the report's citation true in a fresh clone.
!artifacts/gate3-384.jsonl
